#{[#edb/ref 17592186048403 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065063} :activity/id #uuid "6a8803f8-cdad-59a2-a9d6-fe10c0d001fc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065432 :tutorial/content "At first sight, the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x \\sqrt{x+2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nmight not seem like it can be solved using substitution because $x$ is not the derivative of $x+2.$ But in fact, with some clever manipulation, we can indeed solve it using substitution!\n\nSetting\n\n$$\nu=x+2,\n$$\n\nand computing the required derivatives gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int x \\sqrt{x+2} \\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int x \\sqrt{u} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nSo we still have an $x$ in our integrand. However, we can get rid of it by solving for $x$ in the rule $u=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\nx=u-2,\n$$\n\nand using this, the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u= & \\int (u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (u-2){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral by multiplying out the product and integrating the resulting function with respect to $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (u-2){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\int ({u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u-2\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the final step, we substitute $u=x+2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}(x+2)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{(x+2)^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{(x+2)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064080 :question/id #uuid "109a6d6e-8e62-5eac-bfa8-7c059399efe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-457" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2}x(1-2x)^5\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1-2x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x = \\dfrac 1 2 (1-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x(1-2x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}(1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}({u}^{5}-{u}^{6})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{7}{u}^{7})|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}([0-0]-[\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{42}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{168}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064081 :question/id #uuid "a40f6e7e-f4d3-5f3e-bf9d-99c640e36274" :question/math-academy-id "e-458" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overset{u-1}{\\overset{⏞}{{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overset{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u}{\\overset{⏞}{x\\,\\text{d}x}} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065064}}] :activity/title "Further Integration of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064412}] [#edb/ref 17592186048528 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167705} :activity/id #uuid "0cc53ed2-7a2e-52d3-9bd3-6753c5d459d8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166622 :question/id #uuid "ff40db23-6bc1-539b-9f67-3c72c6f448d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8405" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{1} \\sinh {\\left(2x+1\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x=\\dfrac 1 2 \\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -2 & 1 \\\\ u & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{1}\\sinh (2x + 1)dx &= \\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh udu \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}\\cosh u\\right|_{-3}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh (-3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh 3) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Hyperbolic Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166623 :question/id #uuid "94ad0100-80d0-5fd5-9cda-039d9a8bcc47" :question/math-academy-id "e-8406" :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\csch(3x+1)\\coth(3x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=3x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch (3x + 1)\\coth (3x + 1)dx &= \\intcsch u\\coth u \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\intcsch u\\coth udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-csch u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}csch (3x + 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166624 :question/id #uuid "adec9fbb-c33d-5557-b1cd-dfd42d6c594d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8407" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} {\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}}\\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3$. Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d} u = x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -2 & 2 \\\\ u & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}\\, \\textrm{d}x &=\\int_{-2}^{2} \\sinh{x^3}\\cdot x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\int_{-8}^{8} \\sinh{u}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &=\\left.\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cosh u\\Big\\right|_{-8}^{8} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{(-8)}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{8}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Hyperbolic Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166625 :question/id #uuid "1c7e6096-bb20-53a3-a605-42b72948fe98" :question/math-academy-id "e-8408" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu= \\cosh{x^2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\\sinh{x^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = x\\sinh{x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\cdot x\\sinh{x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm d u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid +C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\cosh{x^2}\\right)+ C \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Hyperbolic Functions Using Substitution"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168095 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int \\csch^2(2x+3) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, it is very similar to the basic integral for the hyperbolic cotangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\csch^2 u \\, \\textrm{d}u = -\\coth u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x+3$.\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu=2x+3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch^{2} (2x + 3)dx &= \\intcsch^{2} u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\intcsch^{2} udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\coth u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\coth (2x + 3) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167617}}] :activity/title "Integrating Hyperbolic Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167023}] [#edb/ref 17592186050694 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173013} :activity/id #uuid "1b7424bb-16f3-51a5-8f12-862fc36f5a2a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905 :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906 :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173207 :tutorial/content "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\n    \n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux form of Green's theorem can be summarized in a single picture, as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8ba4b7e853e3b59e8677b0954c3cfae1b9c482750e13a742bd4d45eda8005d.png)" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Green's Theorem in Flux Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173208 :tutorial/content "Green's theorem allows us to write line integrals as double integrals, and we've seen how Green's theorem simplifies circulation calculations. Even more, Green's theorem can also be used to simplify flux calculations!\n\nTo see how, we first recall that the flux of $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) =  P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\mathbf j$ across $C$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s= \\oint\\limits_C P\\, \\textrm{d}y -Q\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is closed and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nNow, let's set $M= -Q$ and $N=P$. Then, by the circulation form of Green's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{C}N\\,\\text{d}y+M\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}M\\,\\text{d}x+N\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂N}{∂x}-\\frac{∂M}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}-(-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}))\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we've obtained the **Green's theorem in flux form**, which we restate below:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} +\\dfrac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can also write Green's theorem in flux form as\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form allows us to convert a flux line integral into an equivalent double integral." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173012}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172181}] [#edb/ref 17592186049930 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176617} :activity/id #uuid "7ae50821-e92a-574f-9179-08660135fa6f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177096 :tutorial/content "The graph of\n\n$$\n{y=2x+7}\n$$\n\nis shown in the picture below. Let's find the equation of the line that passes through $(4,-6)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\n![](images/d8/d80d162b46c39cebddf07394219ec15f4bdbda94831cd66d22da8f285b680d61.png)\n\nSince parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\nwhich is $2$.\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $2$ and passes through the point $(4,-6)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-6) &= 2(x - 4) \\\\\ny + 6 &= 2x - 8 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175736 :question/id #uuid "4dd86f6d-270f-5942-b7cf-df53a0bf4ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14361" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is parallel to the line $y=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5$." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nthat passes through the point $(0,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - 0) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Passing Through a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175738 :question/id #uuid "8d4fd310-7de4-5498-9473-68c1a61fde46" :question/math-academy-id "e-14368" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(3,1)$ and $(6,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(3,1)$ and $(6,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 1}{6 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 1 &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175740 :question/id #uuid "1e0c95a6-24fc-55d1-9c4c-bf87877b3723" :question/math-academy-id "e-14729" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$. Write your answer in **standard form**." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{6 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= \\frac{3}{4}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 1 &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n4y &= 3x - 7 \\\\\n- 3x + 4y &= -7 \\\\\n3x - 4y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Working With Lines in Standard Form"}}] :activity/title "Finding Equations of Parallel Lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176107}]}
